WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
If you want to think more clearly than

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:08.240
99% of people, learn more quickly, and

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.480
master almost anything in the age of AI,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.960
you need to bring back one forgotten

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.640
skill. How to think on paper. I've spent

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.560
decades in boardrooms and tech companies

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.720
worth billions, and the sharpest

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.080
thinkers I know still reach for pen and

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.400
paper. So, in this video, I'm going to

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.160
share a complete system for how to

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.160
think, how to learn, [music] how to

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.680
create using the most powerful thinking

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:37.160
tool you already own. One that costs you

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.280
a dollar. Sure, paper will not replace

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:43.680
your keyboard or AI today, but it can

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.480
make you much harder to replace. So,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.040
let's get started. The first thing we

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.000
want to talk about is why pen is

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.080
mightier than the prompt. Writing is

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.720
slower than typing. Typing [music]

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:58.840
slower than prompting. But, with each

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:02.320
step, we hand off one more layer of our

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:04.839
thinking to a machine. We humans have

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.640
been thinking with our hands for

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:09.280
thousands of years. There is a

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.160
university in Norway, NTNU, and the

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.600
scientists there found that when we

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.280
write on paper, [music]

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.800
the parts of our brain that light up are

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.120
the same parts where ideas, memories,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.760
and learning [music] take place. But,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.820
the world we live in today is very

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.920
different. We prompt more than we

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:31.720
[music] produce. We used to shape ideas

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.560
on paper. Now, we just rent them. We

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:37.440
select them from whatever the machine

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.560
throws at us. As the French philosopher

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.840
Descartes once said, "I think, therefore

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.520
I am." Well, if you outsource your

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.800
thinking, what's left of you? Here's

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.880
what surprised me the most. Writing on

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:53.040
paper literally shapes your thoughts.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.840
That's why the top leaders still think

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.560
on paper. For instance, DaVinci kept

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.040
7,000 pages of handwritten books,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.120
drawings, diagrams, sketches, [music]

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.480
blueprints, whatever he could get his

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.679
hands on. Charles Darwin worked out the

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:10.800
theory of evolution by drawing diagrams.

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:13.080
And, sure, you would say, "Well,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.320
computers weren't invented then, so they

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.640
had to write on paper." But, the same

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:20.640
applies for business leaders and

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.520
thinkers today. From Richard Branson to

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.240
Nobel Prize winning author Toni

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:30.080
Morrison, and from Michelle Obama to Sam

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.800
Altman, their ideas start on a piece of

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.440
paper. And, here's the surprise. On

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.400
paper, you're not just writing, you're

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.560
drawing by hand. And, our brain treats

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.440
it very differently than just typing.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.480
When you type, every keystroke is the

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.800
same motion. [music]

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.960
One of your fingers pressing down A or

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.200
Z, love letter or legal brief, the

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:55.680
physical act of creating each letter is

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.506
identical, which is great for speed.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.640
But, when you write by hand,

00:02:57.506 --> 00:03:00.400
>> [music]

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:02.920
>> every letter is a unique physical

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.560
experience. The pressure of the pen, the

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.960
speed of the stroke, the curve of each

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.400
shape. In neuroscience, this is called

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:14.600
haptic perception. Your brain tags each

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:17.640
idea with a sensory fingerprint. The

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:20.040
thought does not just live on paper. It

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.000
lives in your body. Every letter you

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.120
write gives shape to [music] your

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.640
thoughts. Current science shows that

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:29.602
even doodling seems to lower cortisol

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.640
and reduce performance anxiety.

00:03:29.602 --> 00:03:32.160
>> [music]

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.480
>> And, here's my favorite example. J.K.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.320
Rowling wrote the first chapters of

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.760
Harry Potter by [music] hand in a cafe

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.320
in Edinburgh while she was surviving on

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.680
government benefits, anxious about her

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.720
future. A single mother, single pen, a

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.960
piece of paper, 450 million copies sold.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.880
Now, of course, not everything belongs

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:58.120
on paper. But, once you know when to use

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:01.040
paper, it changes how you think, how you

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.320
create, and how you even feel.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:07.000
Here's the framework I call the three

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:09.360
originals. The first one is invention.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.760
What do you do when you need to generate

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.400
something that doesn't exist in the

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.079
world yet? A new idea, a solution, a

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.200
direction, a strategy. Use paper to

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:23.280
write whatever comes to mind. The rule

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:25.320
here is simple. Create, don't criticize.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.000
While you're generating, tell your

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:32.080
internal judge to take a vacation. Be

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.320
free. Be messy. Write whatever comes to

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:38.560
mind, fragments, flashes of completely

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:40.400
unrelated thoughts, doodles, drawings.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.600
You want to give your brain some

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.440
breathing room so it can fly [snorts]

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.800
through the white space and make

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:49.480
connections it hasn't made before. The

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:53.440
second is introspection. This is where

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:57.400
the fog refuses to lift. I remember when

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:01.080
I used to feel overwhelmed or defeated

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:03.880
or angry or stuck, I would use paper as

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.480
my friend, my external mind. It's hard

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.960
to think your way out of any emotional

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.080
fog. Sometimes, though, you can give it

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.360
a language. Label your feelings. Name

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:18.920
them. Accept them. Navigate the inner

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:22.160
maze. Let the page carry the burden so

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:24.480
you can feel light. Once it's out, you

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.920
can see it. And, once you can see it,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.480
you can move. And, by the way, sometimes

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:31.840
typing feverishly also works for [music]

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.640
me because it captured the stream of

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.280
consciousness. But, usually, I find the

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.948
slower process of writing on paper

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.080
produces much deeper catharsis.

00:05:39.948 --> 00:05:42.680
>> [music]

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.160
>> And, the third is intuition. Einstein

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.640
reportedly said, "If he had 1 hour to

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:49.920
solve a problem, he would spend 55

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.120
minutes defining it and 5 minutes

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.040
solving it." That's first principle

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:57.560
thinking. Untangling the problem itself.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:00.680
What do I actually know to be true? What

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:02.840
am I assuming? How do I formulate this

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:05.160
problem? That's where paper comes handy.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.880
So, invention, introspection, and

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.360
intuition. Now, why do I call them three

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:13.880
originals? Because they are the three

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:17.040
unique traits that make you and me truly

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:20.320
human. No other human or machine can do

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.360
those three steps exactly the same way

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.520
that you'll do them. They are like your

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.080
fingerprints. Now, let's talk about

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.920
staring down the void because maybe

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:32.000
you're thinking, "All right, this sounds

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:35.080
right, but the moment I face a blank

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.680
page, I always freeze." Does it happen

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:40.320
to you? Researchers from Princeton and

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:43.280
UCLA found that students who took notes

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.320
by hand understood concepts more deeply

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.920
than those who type. Now, the typists

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:51.120
recorded more words, but they ended up

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.240
understanding less. More speed with less

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.640
depth. So, if the evidence is this

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:57.880
clear, why don't we use paper all the

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:00.880
time? Because we don't like staring at

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:03.200
the blank page. You know, the blank page

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.000
syndrome is exactly what drives all of

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.640
us to ChatGPT. Type something, [music]

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.720
anything, and within 3 seconds, you're

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.649
going to get three paragraphs. Instant

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.680
relief,

00:07:11.649 --> 00:07:14.920
>> [snorts]

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:18.120
>> but only to the symptom. The underlying

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:21.480
cause never gets addressed because if

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:24.080
you shy away from the most uncomfortable

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:28.320
moment in all of creative and

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:31.080
intellectual work, that blank page, then

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.120
you're shying away from clarity and

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:37.400
originality. In psychology, it's called

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.880
desired difficulty. The harder your

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.760
brain has to work to generate a thought,

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:45.280
the deeper it gets wired in. The strong

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:47.800
resistance is what gives rise to strong

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.760
results. But, doing it without judgment

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.280
is hard. Here's what happens to all of

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.960
us. A friend of mine is one of the best

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:58.560
cooks I know. Cooking is her calling.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:01.000
She loses herself in it, and every dish

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.120
is a masterclass. But, then there are

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:06.160
weeks where she just hates cooking. I

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.160
asked her about it, and she said,

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.840
"That's when my mother-in-law is

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.080
visiting, and she stands right there

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.760
next to me in the kitchen." So, no

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.120
matter how good you are at what you do,

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.000
when you have to do it while being

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.280
judged, there's no chance you're going

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:23.960
to create your masterpiece. So, I think

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.600
the blank page is not our problem. The

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:29.160
real problem is our inner judge that's

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:31.680
staring at it. And, that inner voice is

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.240
telling you that you have nothing new to

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.159
say. Well, don't listen to it. When you

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:40.000
sit down in front of that blank page,

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:43.159
you want to quickly form precise ideas.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.200
Those four words are your four judges.

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:48.560
Quickly form precise ideas. Let's take

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:51.080
each one. First, quickly.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.520
Why rush? What's the rush? Your best

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:56.120
thinking never arrives on schedule. So,

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.400
give it 15 minutes, maybe 20 minutes.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:01.600
Stare at it for a while. The point of

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.400
the paper is to slow you down. Quickly

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.280
is overrated. Second, form.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:09.000
Nothing on this page needs to be

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:11.480
well-formed. Half a thought, good.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:14.280
Disconnected words, great. An arrow

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.520
pointing nowhere, even better. All of it

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:19.680
counts. No one's going to see this piece

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:21.840
of paper but you. Third, precise. Now,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.920
this is the biggest trap for a lot of

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:27.280
us. Be random. Be imprecise. Let it

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:29.560
flow. Even if you haven't found any

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.400
words yet, write them anyway. And

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:34.360
fourth, ideas.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:37.680
It does not have to be a great idea or a

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:41.120
new idea or even an idea.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:43.960
It can be a feeling, a question, a word,

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.920
a phrase, a doodle, a shape. Whatever

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.800
shows up in your head

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.840
is yours. [music]

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.920
So, go ahead and fire all those four

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.520
judges. And, remember, you don't need to

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.760
fill that page. You just have [music] to

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.280
empty your mind. Do that honestly

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.760
enough, and that blank page will take

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:03.880
care of itself. You know, every idea has

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:06.840
a journey and it needs many vehicles,

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:09.000
paper, keyboard, and AI. They're not

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.680
rivals, they're partners. You just need

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:14.040
to build a system to integrate them. The

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:17.200
core question behind the system is not

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:19.920
about which tool is best, it's what your

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.240
idea needs next. So, there are three

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.760
ways to think about it. First, if your

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:28.120
idea needs freedom, go to paper. When

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.760
the idea is still fragile, you know,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:33.600
it's a feeling, a fragment, a question

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:36.920
that won't leave you alone. Still in its

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:39.080
embryonic stage, it needs time and space

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.840
to be born. That's where paper is

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.960
perfect because on paper you can let it

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.720
breathe. Because there is no cursor

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:50.080
blinking at you and waiting for you, no

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:52.680
auto complete, no undo, just you and

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:55.040
your freedom. Second, if your idea needs

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:57.640
form, go to the keyboard.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:01.000
Because you're at a point now when your

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:03.920
idea has a pulse and it needs structure

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:05.720
and some kind of sequence, sentences,

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.960
but you still want to spend time with

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:09.440
it. You want to be alone with it. That's

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:11.640
where keyboard is very good. And

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.480
finally, if the idea needs feedback,

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:17.000
then go to AI by all means. It's your

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.680
collaborator and your co-pilot. You can

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.200
have a dialogue with it. It can

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:23.080
challenge your idea, it can expand it,

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.000
it can pressure test it, it can

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:29.160
recombine it, find what's missing. This

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:31.839
is where deep research is a great tool.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:34.839
Now, this framework is not a sequence.

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:37.600
So, you can interchange keyboard and AI

00:11:34.839 --> 00:11:40.240
in any order of your choice.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:43.880
It all depends on what your idea needs

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:46.920
next. For example, this video started on

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:50.240
this paper, 30 minutes away from any

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:53.520
screen. Just fragments, arrows,

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:56.160
questions I couldn't answer yet. Then I

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:58.839
went to the keyboard for structure, then

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.440
AI for deep research and refinement,

00:11:58.839 --> 00:12:03.800
then back again on paper. When I got

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:06.720
stuck, I doodled on paper, went for a

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:09.200
walk. So, from paper to keyboard to AI

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.240
to paper, you know, the loop continued.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:13.000
Your system [music]

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:15.920
is based on what you need next. Is it

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.440
freedom? Is it form? Is it feedback?

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.680
Your vehicle will change on this journey

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:23.080
accordingly. But in the end, the journey

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:24.839
starts with you and ends [music] with

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:27.440
you. And that's the most important

00:12:24.839 --> 00:12:31.080
takeaway from all of this. AI can

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:34.240
amplify your ideas, expand them, polish

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:37.200
them, even execute your ideas at a scale

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:40.680
you never imagined, but it cannot create

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:43.800
them for you. For that, it's you and

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:47.400
that piece of paper. Today, AI is

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:49.240
already smarter than us in many ways and

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:52.680
intelligence is becoming cheap, it's

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:55.080
becoming a commodity. So, what makes you

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:58.160
irreducibly human?

00:12:55.080 --> 00:13:01.320
Your creation, your emotion, your

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:04.120
intuition. You know, the three originals

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:06.440
and paper protects all three of them.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:09.280
Think of a sculptor. They don't begin

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:11.960
with polish, first comes the rough

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:15.120
shape, you know, the messy first cuts,

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:17.839
all that work that nobody sees. And if

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:20.960
you polish too early, you'll ruin the

00:13:17.839 --> 00:13:23.320
sculpture. That's why thinking on paper

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:27.040
is so crucial because, you know, from

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:31.600
the beginning of human progress, every

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:35.400
giant leap began as a small, innocent,

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:38.120
original idea, but each one of them was

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:42.160
forged in solitude

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:44.440
through messy first cuts. Your ideas are

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:47.920
the same. When you shape them in

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:50.640
solitude, they shape who you become and

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:52.680
they shape the world around you. So,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:55.720
make your first [music] cut. Make it

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:58.320
yourself on a piece of paper before the

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:59.839
world or the machine gets to reshape it

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:01.880
for you.

00:13:59.839 --> 00:14:03.600
Because it's the most human thing you

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:05.280
can do.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.400
If you liked this video, here's the

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:09.440
latest one on how you can have many

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:10.854
interests and still be amazingly

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:10.960
successful.

00:14:10.854 --> 00:14:14.680
>> [music]

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:14.680
>> Thank you and I love you.

